INT. KRVX 91.3 FM - RURAL RADIO STATION - NIGHT

A cinderblock studio in a cornfield. Foam-padded walls, a mixing board with taped-over knobs, a window onto the black field. A red ON AIR light burns.

DEE-DEE HARGROVE, 40s, headphones, cardigan, a thermos of tea, sits at the console. She speaks into the mic in the voice of a lifelong friend.

DEE-DEE
That was Patsy Cline, and you're listening to Night Owls on KRVX. Four a.m. in Calloway County. Roads are clear and the corn is quiet.

She flicks a switch. Music slides in. She leans back.

ROLAND TIBBETTS, 60s, station engineer, in coveralls, enters with a toolbox and a flashlight. He sets the box down next to a cabinet of cables.

ROLAND
Transmitter's running hot again.

DEE-DEE
Is it going to melt?

ROLAND
Not tonight.

DEE-DEE
Then let it be.

He snorts. Sits on a stool. Unwraps a sandwich.

CAL PINKERTON, 20s, intern, lanky, sleepy, shuffles in with an armful of CDs and drops them on the console. He yawns.

CAL
Request line's been quiet. Except one.

DEE-DEE
Who?

CAL
Doesn't give a name.

The studio phone blinks. Dee-Dee pushes a button. Her mic opens to the caller, softly.

DEE-DEE
KRVX, you're on the air.

A pause. Static, then a thin voice, dry as paper.

CALLER (V.O.)
Hello. I'd like to hear "Wild Horses."

DEE-DEE
Of course. Is it for someone?

CALLER (V.O.)
For the three of you.

Dee-Dee looks up. Roland stops chewing. Cal looks at the window.

DEE-DEE
That's very kind. May I ask your name?

CALLER (V.O.)
You can see me.

Out in the black field, a single flashlight clicks on. It points toward the studio. It does not move.

Cal steps to the glass. Roland stands, sandwich forgotten. Dee-Dee keeps her voice steady.

DEE-DEE (CONT'D)
(into mic)
Here's that request, folks.

She slides the fader. The song begins. In the field, the flashlight goes out.
